+# Decision: Change-delivery route
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event and assigns each committed edit one event revision. Those revisions have to reach up to 800 simultaneous browsers per event, within the 15-second freshness target, surviving browser disconnects of up to 20 minutes. The route that carries committed revisions from the server to browsers is a load-bearing choice: it sizes three downstream nodes that cannot be designed until it is settled — the change store (what it must retain and publish), delivery (what sessions or responses it owns), and browser sync (how a browser subscribes, recovers, and catches up).
+
+Event updates carry stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records, so sending them through third-party infrastructure raises no data-sensitivity barrier — that consideration does not drive the choice in any direction.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates an option: all three routes can meet the 15-second freshness target and all preserve one authoritative server-assigned revision, so those are background, not discriminators. The choice turns on four weighted criteria.
+
+- **Vendor independence — major.** Whether the realtime path is coupled to a single external provider's API, SDK, and availability. No current project tenet governs this; the standing tenet permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure. This criterion's weight comes from the stated priority to keep the board free of a realtime-vendor dependency.
+- **Update latency — moderate.** How quickly a committed revision appears in connected browsers. Subsecond updates would be useful but are not required; the 15-second target governs, so latency differences below it are a comfort margin, not a pass/fail.
+- **Operating cost — moderate.** Recurring spend to run the route at 800 connections and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second. Not declared the overriding criterion.
+- **Operational burden — moderate.** Ongoing operations the project must own: connection capacity, reconnect handling, or publication reconciliation. Not declared the overriding criterion.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+- **Description.** Browsers re-request the board on a fixed ~5-second interval over HTTPS. The change store answers cursor queries, returning the revisions committed since the browser's last-seen cursor; delivery stays request/response with no persistent connections; browsers run a polling loop and advance their cursor.
+- **Analysis.**
+  - Vendor independence: **strong** — plain HTTPS, no realtime provider.
+  - Update latency: **weak** — up to the poll interval of lag plus request round-trip; meets the 15-second target but is the laggiest of the three.
+  - Operating cost: **partial** — no vendor fees, but up to 800 browsers polling every few seconds is a steady repeated-read load on the server and store.
+  - Operational burden: **strong** — stateless request/response with no connections to hold or reconnect; the only new store capability is cursor queries.
+- **Summary.** Simplest to operate, at the cost of the most lag and the most repeated reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+- **Description.** The server holds a persistent WebSocket connection per browser and publishes each committed revision to the connections subscribed to that revision's event. The change store retains an ordered, replayable revision history and reliably hands each committed revision to delivery; delivery owns the sessions (connection acceptance, subscriber tracking, publication); browsers subscribe over the socket, treat a dropped connection as a visible stale state, and catch up on missed revisions after reconnecting.
+- **Analysis.**
+  - Vendor independence: **strong** — no realtime provider; the project owns the whole connection layer.
+  - Update latency: **strong** — revisions are pushed on commit, well inside the target.
+  - Operating cost: **partial** — no vendor fees, but the project runs and scales its own connection infrastructure for 800 simultaneous browsers and burst load.
+  - Operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity and all reconnect/catch-up logic, the heaviest operations of the three.
+- **Summary.** Fast and vendor-free, at the price of owning connection operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+- **Description.** A third-party realtime provider carries the fan-out to browsers. The change store reconciles its committed revisions against what the provider has published; delivery wraps the provider's publish API; browsers use the provider's subscription and reconnect SDK.
+- **Analysis.**
+  - Vendor independence: **weak** — the realtime path is coupled to one provider's API, SDK, and availability.
+  - Update latency: **strong** — the provider delivers low-latency push.
+  - Operating cost: **weak** — recurring fees scaling with connections and message volume.
+  - Operational burden: **strong** — the provider absorbs connection capacity and reconnect; the project maintains only publication reconciliation and provider credentials.
+- **Summary.** The least connection operations and low latency, at recurring fees and provider lock-in.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+It wins the single major criterion, vendor independence, while matching the hosted service on latency and beating polling on it. Polling ties on independence but is the weakest on latency and adds a steady repeated-read load that the pushed routes avoid. The hosted service matches on latency and is the lightest to operate, but loses the major criterion and carries recurring fees.
+
+The decisive trade is operational burden against vendor independence. Self-hosted WebSockets are the most burdensome route to run — the project owns connection capacity for up to 800 simultaneous browsers and all reconnect and catch-up logic. That burden is judged moderate and accepted, because it is the price of keeping the realtime path free of any single provider's API, SDK, availability, and recurring fees.
+
+**Tenet standing.** No project tenet currently backs vendor independence; the standing tenet permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure. This decision therefore rests on a stated priority for this path, not a project-wide lean — a later tenet could change how future routing decisions weigh independence.
+
+**What it forecloses.** Offloading connection scaling and reconnect to a vendor; the project carries that operations work itself.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.**
+
+- Change store: retain an ordered, replayable revision history (the seven-day retention goal already applies) and publish each committed revision reliably to delivery.
+- Delivery: accept and track WebSocket connections per event and publish each revision to its subscribers.
+- Browser sync: detect dropped connections as visible stale state and catch up on missed revisions within the 20-minute recovery window.
